<!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
<section class="hero" id="top">
  <div class="wrap hero-grid">
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow">Education infrastructure · est. 2021</div>
      <h1>The internet reaches 5.4 billion people.<br><em>We teach the rest.</em></h1>
      <p>2.6 billion people still live beyond reliable connectivity. Our content packs, mesh sync protocol, and rugged classroom hardware deliver a full modern curriculum with <strong>zero</strong> bars of signal — and quietly catch up the moment any device touches the network.</p>
      <div class="hero-ctas">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#cta">Request a pilot</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#tech">Read the technical brief</a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-note">Deployed in 41 countries · No account required for learners · Open curriculum formats</div>
    </div>
    <div class="img-slot" data-theme="classroom"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ STATS ============ -->
<div class="stats">
  <div class="wrap stats-grid">
    <div class="stat"><b>1.9M</b><span>active learners</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>12,400</b><span>classrooms equipped</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>41</b><span>countries deployed</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>99.2%</b><span>eventual sync success</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============ TRUSTED ============ -->
<div class="trusted">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>Working alongside</p>
    <div class="trusted-row">
      <span>UNESCO-aligned programs</span><span>Ministry of Education pilots</span><span>Rural school districts</span><span>Refugee education NGOs</span><span>Community libraries</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============ OPERATING THESIS (corporate) ============ -->
<section id="thesis">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">Our operating thesis</div>
    <h2>Impact, synergized. Outcomes, actualized.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Untether operationalizes a stakeholder-centric, mission-velocity flywheel that compounds learning equity across the last mile — because the future of education is human-first, signal-optional, and radically scalable.</p>
    <div class="features-grid">
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🚀</span><h3>Mission-velocity frameworks</h3><p>We de-risk ambiguity by aligning cross-functional pods against North-Star metrics, unlocking asymmetric upside for every stakeholder in the value chain.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🤝</span><h3>Stakeholder-centric value loops</h3><p>Our closed-loop feedback architecture converts field signal into actionable insight, driving durable, defensible, community-owned outcomes at planetary scale.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">📈</span><h3>Outcome-aligned growth engines</h3><p>Every workstream ladders up to measurable impact KPIs, ensuring our growth motion stays ruthlessly outcome-aligned and vanity-metric-free.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🌐</span><h3>Radical accessibility, productized</h3><p>We democratize best-in-class pedagogy through a low-lift, high-trust delivery layer engineered for zero-connectivity, high-dignity learning journeys.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">♻️</span><h3>Continuous impact compounding</h3><p>Impact is not an event; it's a flywheel. We reinvest learnings into the next iteration, compounding equity gains quarter over quarter.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🧭</span><h3>Values-led, first-principles execution</h3><p>We think from first principles, move with founder-grade urgency, and hold ourselves accountable to a bias-for-action, learner-obsessed culture.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ PROBLEM ============ -->
<section class="problem" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap problem-grid">
    <div class="img-slot" data-theme="globe"></div>
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow">The problem</div>
      <h2>"Just put it online" is not a strategy for a third of humanity.</h2>
      <p class="lede">EdTech quietly assumes broadband. Where that assumption fails, learning platforms fail with it — silently, and at enormous scale.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>2.6 B</b><span>people have no meaningful internet access, concentrated in rural and low-income regions.</span></li>
        <li><b>463 M</b><span>students couldn't be reached by remote learning during recent school closures — most because of connectivity, not devices.</span></li>
        <li><b>$8–14 / GB</b><span>effective mobile data cost in the least-connected markets, versus pennies in wealthy ones. Streaming video lectures is economically absurd.</span></li>
        <li><b>6–20 hrs / wk</b><span>typical grid electricity availability in many rural schools we serve. Everything we ship is built for intermittent power.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ HOW ============ -->
<section class="how" id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">How it works</div>
    <h2>Pack. Deploy. Sync — whenever the network shows up.</h2>
    <p class="lede">The entire platform is designed around one rule: the network is a bonus, never a dependency.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="num">1</div>
        <h3>Pack the curriculum</h3>
        <p>Educators assemble <strong>Content Packs</strong> — versioned, compressed bundles of lessons, video, exercises, and assessments. A full K-12 year in 3 languages fits in under 48 GB. Packs are signed, deduplicated at the block level, and diffable, so an update ships only what changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="num">2</div>
        <h3>Deploy to the edge</h3>
        <p>A <strong>Hearth Hub</strong> — our solar-tolerant classroom server — broadcasts the pack over local Wi-Fi to any device: donated tablets, teachers' phones, or our e-ink readers. No accounts, no app stores, no SIM cards. A classroom is live 20 minutes after unboxing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="num">3</div>
        <h3>Sync opportunistically</h3>
        <p>Progress, assessments, and teacher notes queue locally. When <em>any</em> device in the mesh touches connectivity — a teacher visiting town, a bus with a hub driving past — the whole classroom's data rides along, encrypted, in kilobytes. We call it <strong>sneakernet with manners</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ PLATFORM / FEATURES ============ -->
<section id="platform">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">The platform</div>
    <h2>Everything a connected classroom has. Nothing a connection requires.</h2>
    <div class="features-grid">
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">📦</span><h3>Content Packs</h3><p>Open-format (ZIM, xAPI, EPUB, H5P) bundles with block-level dedup. Ministries and NGOs can author their own; our tooling validates, compresses, and signs them.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🕸️</span><h3>Hearth Mesh</h3><p>Hubs within radio range form a self-healing mesh and gossip pack updates and progress data peer-to-peer. One connected hub eventually updates an entire district.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🔋</span><h3>Power-realistic hardware</h3><p>Hearth Hub idles at 4 W, runs 14+ hours on its internal battery, and charges from solar, grid, or a motorcycle battery. E-ink readers last 3 weeks per charge.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🧠</span><h3>Adaptive learning, on-device</h3><p>Our spaced-repetition and mastery engine runs entirely locally — a 40 MB model, not a cloud API. Learners get a personalized path with zero connectivity.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🧑‍🏫</span><h3>Teacher dashboard</h3><p>Attendance, mastery heatmaps, and at-risk flags computed on the hub itself. Teachers see this on any browser on the local network — no login wall in front of a lesson, ever.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🔐</span><h3>Privacy by physics</h3><p>Learner data is pseudonymous, encrypted at rest, and never leaves the region's designated sync server. The default state of all data is: it stays in the classroom.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🌍</span><h3>34 languages</h3><p>Full UI and core curriculum localization, including right-to-left scripts and audio-first modes for early or low-literacy readers.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">✍️</span><h3>Offline assessments</h3><p>Timed tests with local integrity checks; results are cryptographically timestamped so certifications remain valid even when submitted weeks later.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><span class="icon">🛠️</span><h3>Repairable by design</h3><p>Standard screws, socketed storage, published schematics, and a spare-parts kit in every district shipment. Median field repair time: 11 minutes.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ TECH DEEP DIVE ============ -->
<section class="tech" id="tech">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">Technical brief</div>
    <h2>The boring, load-bearing details.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Offline-first is not a feature flag. It's a different set of physics. Here is how we handle them.</p>
    <div class="tech-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="tech-block">
          <h3>Delta sync protocol</h3>
          <p>Packs are content-addressed Merkle DAGs. A device advertises its root hashes; peers respond with only missing blocks. A typical monthly curriculum update transfers <code>~140 MB</code> instead of re-shipping <code>48 GB</code>. Transfers resume mid-block after power loss.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tech-block">
          <h3>Conflict resolution</h3>
          <p>Learner progress is modeled as CRDTs (grow-only sets for completions, LWW-registers for preferences, PN-counters for attempt tallies), so weeks-divergent devices merge deterministically with no server arbitration and no lost work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tech-block">
          <h3>Opportunistic transport</h3>
          <p>The sync daemon treats every channel as a courier: Wi-Fi Direct, hub-to-hub 900 MHz radio, USB drives (yes, really — signed and verified), and finally the internet if it appears. Priority queues put assessment results ahead of telemetry, always.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tech-block">
          <h3>Storage honesty</h3>
          <p>Flash on cheap devices lies about writes. We journal everything, checksum every block on read, and keep <code>2</code> rotating pack generations so a corrupt update can never brick a classroom mid-semester.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="img-slot wide" data-theme="circuit" style="margin-bottom:26px"></div>
        <h3 class="sans" style="margin-bottom:6px">Hearth Hub v3 — field specs</h3>
        <table class="spec-table">
          <tr><td>Idle / peak draw</td><td>4 W / 11 W</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Internal battery</td><td>14 h continuous teaching</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Storage</td><td>2 TB (socketed, mirrored)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Concurrent devices</td><td>120 over local Wi-Fi</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mesh radio range</td><td>1.8 km line-of-sight (900 MHz)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Operating temp</td><td>−10 °C to 55 °C, dust-sealed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cold boot to serving</td><td>34 seconds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Field-replaceable parts</td><td>9 (screwdriver included)</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

