<!-- ============ FAQ ============ -->
<section class="faq" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">FAQ</div>
    <h2>Questions we actually get asked.</h2>
    <div style="margin-top:28px">
      <details>
        <summary>Does "offline" mean the content gets stale?</summary>
        <p>No. Packs are versioned and delta-synced: when any device in a mesh touches connectivity, only changed blocks propagate outward — typically ~140 MB for a monthly full-curriculum update. A district's most remote classroom is usually current within two weeks of an update, with no one doing anything on purpose.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What devices do learners need?</summary>
        <p>Anything with a browser: donated tablets, shared family phones, school desktops. The hub serves everything over local Wi-Fi. For device-poor classrooms we supply e-ink readers with 3-week battery life at cost.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do certifications work without a server to verify them?</summary>
        <p>Assessment results are hashed and timestamped on-device against the hub's secure clock, then countersigned when they eventually reach the regional sync server. The certificate is verifiable from the paper printout alone — a QR code contains the signed proof.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Who owns the learner data?</summary>
        <p>The school, then the ministry — never us. Data is pseudonymous by default, stays on regional infrastructure, and every hub has a one-button full export in open formats. Our contracts forbid us from monetizing learner data, and our architecture makes it mostly impossible anyway.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What happens when a hub breaks 400 km from a technician?</summary>
        <p>Nine parts are field-replaceable with the included screwdriver, guided by an illustrated repair booklet (and an offline video series on the hub itself — which mirrors to neighboring hubs, so the repair guide survives the failure it documents). Median field repair: 11 minutes.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Are you open source?</summary>
        <p>The sync protocol, pack format, and hardware schematics are published. The adaptive-learning engine is source-available for ministries and auditors. We think infrastructure for public education should be inspectable by the public that depends on it.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

